<div class="callout"><div class="ct">Who actually emerges. The single biggest honest earner on the board, both all-time and this week, is <strong>abs_veto_55s</strong>. The one above it this week (grind_fast) and the family's siblings (abs_veto_50s/60s) are all just research variants of gates the desk already runs &mdash; grind_fast is the uncapped-chandelier cousin of the live grind_long, the 50s/60s are the same veto with a shorter/longer confirm window. Among strategies that are <em>not yet on the live desk</em>, abs_veto_55s is #1 in both windows by a clear margin. It nominates itself. Two useful facts fall straight out of the family ordering: 55s &gt; 50s &gt; 60s in honest dollars, so the 55-second confirm is the sweet spot (not an arbitrary pick), and the raw un-vetoed thrust it is built on (thrust_loose) makes essentially nothing (+$131 over 424 trades, +$0.3 each) &mdash; so every dollar abs_veto earns is the <em>veto</em> doing the work, not the underlying thrust.</div></div>
</div>

<div class="card">
<h3>The promotion battery &mdash; abs_veto_55s, run cold on this week's data</h3>
<p>abs_veto_55s is thrust_loose plus a 55-second continuation confirm: it takes a raw thrust signal, waits 55 seconds, and only fires if the burst is still going &mdash; which throws away roughly 44% of the raw thrusts as fake-outs. Here is the full robustness read, not just the headline number.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th class="ln">Test</th><th class="ln">Result</th><th class="num">verdict</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">Headline (all-time)</td><td class="ln">238 trades, <strong>+$2,668</strong>, 47% win, +$11.2/trade, worst day &minus;$148, max drawdown &minus;$441</td><td class="num"><span class="tag">strong</span></td></tr>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">Spread across days</td><td class="ln"><strong>9 of 12 days green.</strong> Best +$761 (07-23), worst &minus;$148 (07-22). Not one lucky session.</td><td class="num"><span class="tag">broad</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="ln">Concentration check</td><td class="ln">Top 3 days (07-28 +$791, 07-23 +$676, 07-17 +$581) = $2,048 of the $2,668 (77%). Strip all three and the remaining nine days still net <strong>+$620</strong>.</td><td class="num"><span class="tag">survives strip-3</span></td></tr>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">Regime split (chop vs trend, by entry ER)</td><td class="ln">Chop (ER&lt;0.18): 112 tr, <strong>+$872</strong>, +$7.8/tr. Trend (ER&ge;0.18): 126 tr, <strong>+$1,796</strong>, +$14.3/tr. Green in <em>both</em>, roughly twice as good in trend.</td><td class="num"><span class="tag">both regimes</span></td></tr>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">Walk-forward (first half vs second)</td><td class="ln">First 119 trades +$1,250; second 119 trades +$1,418. Slightly <em>better</em> late &mdash; no decay.</td><td class="num"><span class="tag">stable</span></td></tr>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">Head-to-head vs un-vetoed thrust_loose</td><td class="ln">abs_veto_55s +$2,668 vs thrust_loose +$131 on the same board. The veto's incremental edge is <strong>+$2,538</strong>.</td><td class="num"><span class="tag">the veto is the edge</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>And the operator's key point from last week held up when re-tested cold: <strong>it is a genuine two-sided gate and both sides earn their keep.</strong> The live desk only runs the short side (thrust_short), leaving the long side on the table.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th class="ln">abs_veto_55s side</th><th class="num">N</th><th class="num">net $</th><th class="num">win%</th><th class="num">$/trade</th><th class="num">max DD</th><th class="ln">vs raw thrust</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">LONG (all-time)</td><td class="num">122</td><td class="num">+1,187</td><td class="num">47</td><td class="num">+9.73</td><td class="num">&minus;456</td><td class="ln">+$1,563 better</td></tr>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">SHORT (all-time)</td><td class="num">116</td><td class="num">+1,482</td><td class="num">47</td><td class="num">+12.77</td><td class="num">&minus;331</td><td class="ln">+$974 better</td></tr>
<tr><td class="ln">LONG (this week)</td><td class="num">66</td><td class="num">+776</td><td class="num">50</td><td class="num">+11.75</td><td class="num">&minus;310</td><td class="ln">&mdash;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="ln">SHORT (this week)</td><td class="num">63</td><td class="num">+659</td><td class="num">44</td><td class="num">+10.46</td><td class="num">&minus;331</td><td class="ln">&mdash;</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>The short-only live-slot mirror A/B says the same thing in the live slot's own shape (short-only + chandelier): <strong>thrust_short_absveto55 made +$318 (53 tr, 45% win)</strong> against <strong>thrust_short_raw's &minus;$137 (94 tr, 33% win)</strong> &mdash; the veto turns the desk's live short thrust from a slow bleed into a real earner.</p>

<div class="callout"><div class="ct"><strong>Verdict: PROMOTE, and promote it TWO-SIDED. <span class="tag">promote 2-sided</span></strong> Every robustness cut it can be put through &mdash; per-day spread, strip-the-best-3, chop-vs-trend, both walk-forward halves, head-to-head vs its own un-vetoed base &mdash; comes back positive, and both directions earn independently. Honest caveats: it is still only a ~two-week, single-summer-regime sample, and three good days carry most of the total (though it stays green with those three removed). The real out-of-sample test is the forward paper run &mdash; the shadow nominates, the paper desk decides. But of everything on the board, this is the one that has earned a live slot, and clipping it to short-only leaves roughly half its proven money on the floor.</div></div>
</div>

<div class="card">
<h3>Relegation view &mdash; tune the SIDE, never bin a direction</h3>
<p>The standing rule is that a gate runs both ways with its two sides tuned independently, so nothing here is a recommendation to retire a direction &mdash; it is a read on <em>which side of which gate needs what</em>, from this week's fresh per-side numbers. Every "bleed" below is a side-and-regime problem, not a broken gate.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th class="ln">Gate</th><th class="ln">This week, per side (honest $)</th><th class="ln">Diagnosis</th><th class="num">action</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">rgv</td><td class="ln">LONG bleeds hard (rg_long_fast &minus;$667, rg_long_20_t20 &minus;$520). SHORT holds up (rg_short_025_flow50 <strong>+$71</strong>, rg_short_025_raw +$28, rg_short_050 +$11).</td><td class="ln">Classic two-sided split &mdash; the sides want opposite treatment. Short has a stable tight-ext green zone; long has no filter that keeps it green, it's a <em>router</em> problem (don't fade into a proven up-move).</td><td class="num"><span class="tag">tune side: rgv SHORT tight-ext base; rgv LONG = router-only</span></td></tr>
<tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">capitulation</td><td class="ln">Unfiltered fader (capit_loose) &minus;$1,455 this week, &minus;$5,250 all-time, 17&ndash;21% win.</td><td class="ln">The loose, unfiltered form is the worst thing on the board. The deployed capitulation_long uses require_flip, which is the actual fix &mdash; the bleed is the <em>unfiltered</em> version, not the gate.</td><td class="num"><span class="tag">keep require_flip + router guard</span></td></tr>
<tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">thrust (short)</td><td class="ln">Raw short (thrust_short_raw) &minus;$463 this week, 31% win.</td><td class="ln">Not a direction to bin &mdash; the same short direction with the 55s veto (thrust_short_absveto55) is +$318. The fix is the entry veto, which is the promotion above.</td><td class="num"><span class="tag">fix = the abs_veto veto</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="ln">exhaustion (short)</td><td class="ln">Fixed-target shadow (exhaustion_rev) &minus;$302, but that shadow uses a fixed 12pt target it never trades to. LIVE exhaustion_short is the desk's cleanest fader (see the exit lab below).</td><td class="ln">Do not judge this gate by its fixed-target shadow &mdash; the live chandelier version rides the fade body. Judge it on live fills.</td><td class="num"><span class="tag">keep, judge live</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<div class="callout"><div class="ct">Nothing gets relegated. Every red line above is a side that needs a filter, a regime guard, or the router &mdash; not a direction to switch off. That is the whole point of the two-sided rule, and this week's per-side numbers back it: rgv's short side is quietly green while its long side bled, on the same gate, in the same days.</div></div>
</div>

<div class="card">
<h3>Grind exit shadow &mdash; two-ratchet runner-clip watch</h3>
<p>Background: grind_long deployed the pure-6.0 threshold-chandelier as its exit. A second "mop-up" ratchet was tested three ways and rejected every time, because the reverser money is tangled up with the runner-retrace zone it would clip. It stays dead but is <em>shadowed</em> to catch the one thing the flat summer archive can't produce: a real runner getting clipped on a genuine trend day, since the big rMFE&ge;4R runners carry the bulk of grind's edge.</p>

