<div class="callout"><div class="ct"><strong>Verdict: no clip fired, and far too thin to reopen the case. <span class="tag">stays dead, keep shadowing</span></strong> The honest facts: grind barely traded live this window (4 fills, all on one day 07-27), so this is a single-day, one-runner read, not the "genuine trend week" that would count as evidence. On the one runner it saw, the two-ratchet did <em>not</em> clip it &mdash; it actually rode it for <em>more</em> ($145 shadow vs $23 live, +$122). That is mildly interesting (the mop-up arm added to a runner rather than cutting it), but one runner is nothing to decide on. The finding that would kill it (a real clip) did not fire; the evidence that would revive it (a clean trend week with several untouched runners) hasn't arrived. It stays dead and shadowed until a real trend week gives it a proper test.</div></div>
</div>

<div class="card">
<h3>Grind exit shadow &mdash; 2R-partial smoothness</h3>
<p>The 2R-partial is the only exit lever that survived earlier scrutiny: Lot A banks a 2R scalp for certainty, Lot B rides the pure-6.0 chandelier for the tail. Because grind is base-size 2 it's free to run. It was framed as a <em>variance</em> lever (smoother equity for a small mean give-up), not a mean-raiser &mdash; so it's the operator's call, not a pass/fail.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th class="ln">Metric (07-26 &rarr; 08-01)</th><th class="num">Baseline (deployed 2-lot chandelier)</th><th class="num">2R-partial (shadow)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">Net $</td><td class="num">&minus;278</td><td class="num">&minus;214 &nbsp;(+$64)</td></tr>
<tr><td class="ln">Mean &Delta; per trade (partial &minus; base)</td><td class="num">&mdash;</td><td class="num">+$64.5 (all on the 1 runner)</td></tr>
<tr><td class="ln">Daily std $</td><td class="num">0 (1 day)</td><td class="num">0 (1 day)</td></tr>
<tr><td class="ln">Max drawdown $</td><td class="num">&minus;278</td><td class="num">&minus;214</td></tr>
<tr><td class="ln">Worst day $</td><td class="num">&minus;278</td><td class="num">&minus;214</td></tr>
<tr><td class="ln">Green-day % / green-week %</td><td class="num">0 / 0</td><td class="num">0 / 0</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Where the +$64 came from, trade by trade: on the three losers the partial is identical to baseline (both stop out); the whole difference is the one runner, where banking Lot A at 2R (+$130.5) beat letting both lots ride the chandelier back down (+$66).</p>

<div class="callout"><div class="ct"><strong>Verdict: no new evidence to make the call on. <span class="tag">operator's choice, unchanged</span></strong> The smoothness case is a claim about <em>daily variance and drawdown across many days</em> &mdash; and this window has one grind day and four trades, so daily std is zero and the whole variance thesis is untestable right now. What we can say: this week the partial edged the baseline by $64, entirely by scalping the single runner rather than riding it. That is the opposite of a mean give-up (this week it was a mean <em>gain</em>), purely because the lone runner topped out at 4.2R and the chandelier gave the tail back. One day proves nothing either way; the mean-vs-smoothness choice waits for a multi-day trend sample.</div></div>
</div>

<h2><span class="n">PART 2 &middot; LAB</span> The Regime-Flex Exit Lab</h2>
<p class="lead">The desk's live control is now two files: gate_switches.env decides which gates are on, and exit_overrides.json decides the exit style per gate. This lab does two jobs &mdash; grade the live exhaustion_short fade-scalp trial on its actual fills, and re-prove the operator's regime-graded exit ladder by repricing every gate's real entries on the forward tick/5s path, segmented by regime rung, so the deployable exit_overrides policy is proven rather than guessed.</p>

<div class="card">
<h3>(a) The live exhaustion_short fade-scalp trial &mdash; on its actual fills</h3>
<p>Deployed ~07:55 UTC on 07-31: Lot A banks at 0.5R, Lot B at 1.5R, replacing the prior A@2.5R + chandelier. Here is literally every leg it has filled since.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th class="ln">Time (UTC)</th><th class="ln">leg</th><th class="ln">exit</th><th class="num">$</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">07-31 13:52</td><td class="ln">A (0.5R)</td><td class="ln">TARGET</td><td class="num">+51.5</td></tr>
<tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">07-31 13:52</td><td class="ln">B (1.5R)</td><td class="ln">STOP</td><td class="num">&minus;123.5</td></tr>
<tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">07-31 18:41</td><td class="ln">A (0.5R)</td><td class="ln">STOP</td><td class="num">&minus;27.5</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<div class="callout"><div class="ct"><strong>Verdict: keep running &mdash; two signals is not a grade. <span class="tag">too thin to judge</span></strong> The trial has fired exactly two signals (three legs) for a net of &minus;$99.5. The one thing you can see: on the 13:52 signal the fast 0.5R Lot A banked (+$51.5) while the 1.5R Lot B rode past its cushion and stopped (&minus;$123.5) &mdash; i.e. the fade reversed after the quick scalp, which is exactly the case the "bank A early" logic is meant for, except Lot B still gave it back. The 18:41 signal stopped on the A leg. For honest comparison the prior era (07-27&ndash;07-31, A@2.5R + chandelier, 37 trades) netted +$365 with a healthy 11 targets / 10 chandeliers / 16 stops mix. Two signals cannot overturn that &mdash; let it run and re-grade with real n.</div></div>
</div>

<div class="card">
<h3>(b) Proving the exit ladder &mdash; best exit per gate &times; regime rung</h3>
<p>I repriced every gate's real entries on the forward 5-second path, segmented by the operator's four rungs (BIG-TREND = 30-min ER&ge;0.50, MED-TREND 0.30&ndash;0.50, SCALP-CHOP otherwise, STAY-OUT = violent ATR&ge;22), sweeping a full grid of Lot-A &times; Lot-B scalp-R combos plus wide/tight chandelier, and scored each config only on its home segment. The table is the <em>proven</em> best exit vs the ladder <em>guess</em>.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th class="ln">Gate family</th><th class="ln">rung (n)</th><th class="ln">ladder guess</th><th class="ln">proven best (mean $/tr)</th><th class="num">verdict</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">grind (momentum)</td><td class="ln">MED-TREND (78)</td><td class="ln">1.5 / 2.5</td><td class="ln"><strong>scalp 0.5 / 1.0</strong> (+$10.3, 59% win)</td><td class="num"><span class="tag">guess wrong: go TIGHT</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="ln">grind (momentum)</td><td class="ln">SCALP-CHOP (542)</td><td class="ln">1 / 2</td><td class="ln">scalp 0.5 / 1.0 (&minus;$0.5, 53% win)</td><td class="num"><span class="tag">tight, ~flat</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="ln">grind (momentum)</td><td class="ln">BIG-TREND (5)</td><td class="ln">2.5 / wide-chandelier</td><td class="ln">wideChand &times;2 (+$12.0) &mdash; n=5</td><td class="num"><span class="tag">UNTESTED</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="ln">grind (momentum)</td><td class="ln">STAY-OUT (107)</td><td class="ln">flat</td><td class="ln">every exit loses (best &minus;$3.7)</td><td class="num"><span class="tag">stay flat, confirmed</span></td></tr>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">exhaustion (fader)</td><td class="ln">SCALP-CHOP (216)</td><td class="ln">0.5 / 1.5</td><td class="ln"><strong>tightChand &times;2</strong> (+$2.2, 37% win)</td><td class="num"><span class="tag">tight chandelier &gt; scalp</span></td></tr>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">exhaustion (fader)</td><td class="ln">MED-TREND (90)</td><td class="ln">0.5 / 1.5</td><td class="ln"><strong>scalp 1.5 / 2.5</strong> (+$3.8, 46% win)</td><td class="num"><span class="tag">wider than the guess</span></td></tr>
<tr class="row-hl"><td class="ln">exhaustion (fader)</td><td class="ln">BIG-TREND (15)</td><td class="ln">tight-chandelier</td><td class="ln">tightChand &times;2 (+$26.7, 53% win)</td><td class="num"><span class="tag">holds (thin)</span></td></tr>
<tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">thrust / abs_veto raw</td><td class="ln">every rung (25&ndash;333)</td><td class="ln">1.5 / 2.5</td><td class="ln">bleeds in ALL: BIG &minus;$13, MED &minus;$36, CHOP &minus;$39, STAY &minus;$67</td><td class="num"><span class="tag">exit can't save it</span></td></tr>
<tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">rgv (fader)</td><td class="ln">0.5 / 1.5</td><td class="ln">every rung red (best &minus;$6.9 MED, &minus;$8.7 CHOP, &minus;$27 BIG)</td><td class="ln">&mdash;</td><td class="num"><span class="tag">exit can't save it</span></td></tr>
<tr class="row-bad"><td class="ln">capitulation (fader)</td><td class="ln">0.5 / 1.5</td><td class="ln">MED &minus;$19, CHOP &minus;$20; only tiny BIG (n=8) green</td><td class="ln">&mdash;</td><td class="num"><span class="tag">exit can't save it</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<div class="callout"><div class="ct"><strong>What survived, in plain English.</strong> Two gates respond to the exit and three do not.
<br>&bull; <strong>grind wants a TIGHT scalp, not the ladder's wider guess</strong> &mdash; in medium trend, 0.5/1.0 made +$10.3/trade at a 59% hit-rate; the guessed 1.5/2.5 was one of the worst cells. Grind banks fast or gives it back.
<br>&bull; <strong>exhaustion is the one clean fader</strong> &mdash; a tight chandelier in chop (+$2.2) and in big trend (+$26.7 on 15), and a slightly <em>wider</em> 1.5/2.5 scalp in medium trend (+$3.8), not the 0.5/1.5 guess.
<br>&bull; <strong>thrust, rgv and capitulation bleed in every regime no matter what exit you put on them.</strong> That is the headline finding: for these three, the exit is not the lever &mdash; the lever is the entry (the abs_veto veto is literally the proof of that: same thrust signal, filtered entry, turns &minus;$137 into +$318) and which gates you run at all via gate_switches.env.</div></div>

<div class="callout"><div class="ct"><strong>&#9733; CRITICAL caveat &mdash; BIG-TREND is still not properly tested.</strong> The two-week V7 tape was chop-dominated, so the BIG-TREND rung is tiny in every family (grind n=5, exhaustion/rgv n=15, capitulation n=8; thrust the only semi-real one at n=25 and it bled). The "RIDE" rung &mdash; grind 2.5&ndash;3.5R + wide lock-chandelier on a monster up-day &mdash; is effectively <strong>UNPROVEN</strong> (grind's BIG cell is 5 trades). I did <em>not</em> manufacture a BIG-TREND sample from the retired V5 archive: it is a different desk, contract era and gate set, and repricing V7 gate entries on it would be a fabricated comparison, not an honest one. The only honest tick tape available (V7 capture.db) spans 07-15 to 07-31 and simply does not contain a clean trend week for grind. That remains the #1 unturned stone &mdash; the RIDE rung must be proven on the next genuine trend week (live-forward or a matching-instrument archive), and until then grind's BIG-TREND exit is a guess wearing an n=5 hat.</div></div>

<div class="card">
<h3>The deployable exit_overrides policy (what this proves out to)</h3>
<p>Reading the survivors straight into the per-regime policy &mdash; only the cells with real n and a real edge become overrides; the rest default or are handled by turning the gate off:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th class="ln">Gate</th><th class="ln">BIG-TREND</th><th class="ln">MED-TREND</th><th class="ln">SCALP-CHOP</th><th class="ln">STAY-OUT</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td class="ln">grind_long</td><td class="ln">wide-chandelier <em>(unproven, n=5)</em></td><td class="ln">scalp 0.5 / 1.0</td><td class="ln">scalp 0.5 / 1.0</td><td class="ln">FLAT (gate off)</td></tr>
<tr><td class="ln">exhaustion_short</td><td class="ln">tight-chandelier</td><td class="ln">scalp 1.5 / 2.5</td><td class="ln">tight-chandelier</td><td class="ln">FLAT (gate off)</td></tr>
<tr><td class="ln">thrust / abs_veto</td><td class="ln" colspan="4">exit is not the lever &mdash; keep the abs_veto entry veto; manage via gate on/off, not exit style</td></tr>
<tr><td class="ln">rgv / capitulation</td><td class="ln" colspan="4">exit is not the lever &mdash; per-side filter + router; leave exit at default</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Note the live exit_overrides.json currently sets exhaustion_short to 0.5/1.5 and abs_veto to 1.5/2.5. This lab says exhaustion_short should be regime-graded (tight-chandelier in chop, 1.5/2.5 in med) rather than a flat 0.5/1.5, and that abs_veto's dollars come from its entry veto, not its exit R's &mdash; a candidate tweak for the operator, on a two-week in-sample read, to confirm forward.</p>
</div>

<p class="lead">Bottom line for the week: one thing earned a promotion (abs_veto_55s, two-sided), the two grind-exit shadows produced nothing decisive because grind barely traded (single-day, one-runner), the live exhaustion fade-scalp is two signals old and ungradeable, and the exit lab's cleanest result is a warning as much as a recipe &mdash; grind and exhaustion respond to a tighter, regime-graded exit, but thrust/rgv/capitulation prove that for them the exit is a red herring and the real lever is the entry filter and which gates you let trade. And the honest gap that stays open is BIG-TREND: the desk still has not seen a clean trend week to prove the RIDE rung.</p>
